hypotheses.statement:
In a CAT(0) space, geodesic segments are unique and depend continuously on their endpoints. If $\alpha,\beta:[0,1]\to X$ are geodesics parametrized proportionally to length, then $$\label{eq:distance-convexity}
 d(\alpha(t),\beta(t))
 \le (1-t)d(\alpha(0),\beta(0))+t d(\alpha(1),\beta(1)).$$ If $m$ is the midpoint of $[v,w]$, then $$\label{eq:midpoint-comparison}
 d(z,m)^2\le \frac{d(z,v)^2+d(z,w)^2}{2}
                    -\frac{d(v,w)^2}{4}.$$ For any four points $p,q,r,s$, $$\label{eq:quadrilateral}
 d(p,r)^2+d(q,s)^2
 \le d(p,q)^2+d(q,r)^2+d(r,s)^2+d(s,p)^2.$$

Suppose a group acting geometrically on a CAT(0) space contains elements $s_1,\ldots,s_{39}$ satisfying the $B_{40}$ relations, with $\ell(s_1^2)>0$. Then $$\label{eq:braid-angle-bound}
 \left(\frac{\ell((s_1s_2)^3)}{3\ell(s_1^2)}\right)^2
 \ge\frac{143}{513}>\frac14,$$ and in particular $$\frac{\ell((s_1s_2)^3)}{3\ell(s_1^2)}
 =\frac{\ell((s_1s_2)^3)}{3\ell(s_2^2)}>\frac12.$$

The element $L_j$ commutes with $s_i$ for $i\le j-2$, and $Z_r$ commutes with every $s_i$ for $i<r$. In particular, $$Z_2=s_1^2,\qquad Z_3=(s_1s_2)^3.$$ The element $\delta=s_1\cdots s_{l-1}$ satisfies $$\label{eq:braid-shift}
 \delta s_i\delta^{-1}=s_{i+1}\qquad(1\le i\le l-2).$$ In any geometric action of the ambient group, $$\label{eq:braid-central-pairing}
 \langle s_i^2,Z_r\rangle=\frac{\ell(Z_r)^2}{N_r}
 \qquad(2\le r\le l,\ 1\le i<r).$$

There is no geometric action of $T$ such that, for every $(u,v)\in\mathcal E$, $$\label{eq:strict-ratios}
 \frac{\ell(z_{uv})}{3\ell(u^2)}
 =\frac{\ell(z_{uv})}{3\ell(v^2)}>\frac12.$$

The formulas $$u\cdot\xi=e_{D_u}+r_u\xi\qquad(u=a,b,c)$$ define an affine isometric action of $T$ on $E$. If $V(t)=t\cdot0$, then $$\label{eq:mirror-lipschitz}
 \|V(t)-V(s)\|_1\le d_T(s,t).$$ For each $(u,v)\in\mathcal E$, the elements $u^2,z_{uv}$ act by pure translations with vectors $$\label{eq:mirror-translations}
 L_u=2e_{D_u},\qquad
 L_{uv}=2(e_{D_u}+e_{D_v}+e_{D_{uv}}),$$ where $D_{uv}$ is the third mirror at $D_u\cap D_v$. The three lines $D_{ab},D_{bc},D_{ca}$ are distinct from one another and from $D_a,D_b,D_c$.

Each of these six seminorms is positive definite. Each sector angle $\theta$ satisfies $0<\theta<\pi/3$. The sectors glue isometrically along their shared rays, in the cyclic order $$\label{eq:ray-order}
 a^2,\ z_{ab},\ b^2,\ z_{bc},\ c^2,\ z_{ca},\ a^2.$$ Their radius-one disk $P$ is a topological disk with total angle $0<\Omega<2\pi$. It has a continuous injective map $v_*:P\to E$ whose value in a sector for $U=u^2,H=z_{uv}$ is $$\label{eq:separating-coordinates}
 v_*(\lambda,\mu)=\lambda L_u+\mu L_{uv},$$ and similarly with $v$ in place of $u$.

There are continuous maps $f_n:P\to Y$, with $f_n(0)=y$, and a constant $C_0$ independent of $n,p$, such that $$\label{eq:grid-error}
 d_Y(f_n(p),t_n(p)y)\le C_0.$$ For two points in any one sector, $$\label{eq:sector-convergence}
 \frac{d_Y(f_n(p),f_n(p'))}{n}
 \longrightarrow\|p-p'\|_{U,H}$$ uniformly. In addition, uniformly on $P$, $$\label{eq:radial-coordinates}
 \frac{d_Y(y,f_n(p))}{n}\longrightarrow r(p),
 \qquad
 \frac{V(t_n(p))}{n}\longrightarrow v_*(p).$$ If $A_1,A_2\subset P$ are disjoint nonempty closed sets, then for some $\delta>0$ and all sufficiently large $n$, $$\label{eq:region-separation}
 d_Y(f_n(A_1),f_n(A_2))\ge\delta n.$$

